  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nFrom [15.5.3 Button layout](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/rendering.html#button-layout):\r\n\r\n> If the element is a [button](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/form-elements.html#the-button-element) element,  then the ['display'](https://drafts.csswg.org/css2/#display-prop) property is expected to act as follows:\r\n>  * If the computed value of ['display'](https://drafts.csswg.org/css2/#display-prop) is 'inline-grid', 'grid', 'inline-flex', or 'flex', then behave as the computed value.\r\n> * Otherwise, if the computed value of ['display'](https://drafts.csswg.org/css2/#display-prop) is a value such that the [outer display type](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-display/#outer-display-type) is 'inline', then behave as 'inline-block'.\r\n> * Otherwise, behave as 'flow-root'.\r\n\r\nThis seems to suggest that `<button>` elements in button layout are not affected by `display: none` ?\r\nFirefox does not render buttons with a computed `display` value of `none` (as I would expect).\r\n\r\nIs this simply omitted here because it \"seems obvious enough\"?\r\n",
